# ci-macos-case-mirror: a case-note guard asked the mirror whether the filesystem folds case

**Severity**: MEDIUM — a guard fails on a filesystem that folds case (macOS); the code is right.
**Status**: Fixed (the guard); awaiting the next CI run on macOS
**Branch**: — (default-branch mode; fixes land on master)
**Commit**: this commit (`Fix ci-macos-case-mirror`)
**Reviewer**: none — found by GitHub CI run 45520218555 at `e2c2e08d`, 2026-11-25 — the run after the first's seven fixes, which reached the test binaries the first never ran; for the next openreview.

## Evidence
`case_note::the_line_claims_only_what_the_mirror_then_does` (macOS): `["[Aa]"], as typed: b`, true where false was expected.

The guard skips its as-typed checks where the filesystem folds case, and it learned that from `dst/KEEP.1`, which exists only where the mirror copied `keep.1`. The case with `--include '{a,b}'` leaves `keep.1` out, so on APFS, which folds, the guard read "no fold". It then expected `B` kept and `b` gone, which there are one entry.

## Intake
Admitted. The fault is the guard's assumption of a platform, not the code under it.

## What
The guard asks the filesystem itself: it writes `fold-probe` and looks for `FOLD-PROBE`.

## Guard proof
- Checks:
  - native, Windows and macOS (`CARGO_FEATURE_PURE=1`) clippy are clean;
  - all 16 `case_note` guards pass on Linux, where nothing folds, as before.
- Proof is the next CI run on macOS: this host's filesystems do not fold case.

## Files changed
- `crates/blit-cli/tests/case_note.rs` — the guard.

## Known gaps
- On a filesystem that folds, the guard still checks only the line, not what each mirror kept. That is as before for every case.
        includes: &'static [&'static str],
        line: &'static str,
        /// Destination-only entries, with whether each is kept as typed
        /// and whether it is kept with `--ignore-case`.
        entries: &'static [(&'static str, bool, bool)],
    }
    for case in [
        Case {
            excludes: &["**//Cache"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"**//Cache\" matches exactly as typed; capital letters matter \
                   (--ignore-case ignores them).",
            entries: &[("cache", false, false), ("Cache", false, false)],
        },
        Case {
            excludes: &["Keep", "[!a].dat"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"[!a].dat\" matches exactly as typed, and 1 more pattern matches only as \
                   typed; capital letters matter (--ignore-case changes what they match: [!a] \
                   would stop matching A).",
            entries: &[
                ("A.dat", true, false),
                ("b.dat", true, true),
                ("keep", false, true),
            ],
        },
        // cr-ecm8-2: a letter that is neither the lower nor the upper case
        // of the ones written. The line was absent; the mirror deleted `ς`,
        // which `--ignore-case` keeps.
        Case {
            excludes: &["{Σ,σ}"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"{Σ,σ}\" matches {Σ,σ} only; capital letters matter (--ignore-case would \
                   also match ς).",
            entries: &[("ς", false, true), ("σ", true, true)],
        },
        // cr-ecm8-2: a class beside the letters. The line was absent; the
        // mirror deleted `C`. Not every name can be asked about, so the
        // line states the rule and claims no more.
        Case {
            excludes: &["{[a-c],A,B}"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"{[a-c],A,B}\" matches exactly as typed; capital letters matter \
                   (--ignore-case ignores them).",
            entries: &[("C", false, true), ("B", true, true), ("c", true, true)],
        },
        // cr-ecm8-2: the narrowing was credited to `[!a]` and `A`; `A.1`
        // stays kept, and `B.2` is what the option stops keeping.
        Case {
            excludes: &["{[!a].1,A.1,[!b].2}"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"{[!a].1,A.1,[!b].2}\" matches exactly as typed; capital letters matter \
                   (--ignore-case changes what it matches: [!b] would stop matching B).",
            entries: &[
                ("A.1", true, true),
                ("B.2", true, false),
                ("x.1", true, true),
            ],
        },
        // cr-ecm9-2: one real loss is enough, and it is shown first. The
        // line gave `Keep`'s spellings; `A.txt` stays kept by the
        // alternative beside the class, and `B.txt` is what the option
        // stops keeping.
        Case {
            excludes: &["Keep", "{[!ab].txt,A.txt}"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"{[!ab].txt,A.txt}\" matches exactly as typed, and 1 more pattern matches \
                   only as typed; capital letters matter (--ignore-case changes what they \
                   match: [!ab] would stop matching B).",
            entries: &[
                ("A.txt", true, true),
                ("B.txt", true, false),
                ("keep", false, true),
            ],
        },
        // cr-ecm9-2: a gain is judged against the whole filter. For `a`
        // the line offered `A`, which the pattern beside it keeps already;
        // what the option changes is `x.doc`.
        Case {
            excludes: &["a", "A", "*.Doc"],
            includes: &[],
            line: "\"*.Doc\" matches .Doc only, and 2 more patterns match only as typed; \
                   capital letters matter (--ignore-case would also match .doc, .DOC).",
            entries: &[("A", true, true), ("a", true, true), ("x.doc", false, true)],
        },
        // cr-ecm10-1: beside `./A` there was no line at all — `./A` keeps
        // `A` at the root, where the name was tried — and the mirror
        // deleted `sub/A`, which the option keeps. The line stays, in
        // words that claim nothing about any one name.
        Case {
807:| ci-windows-missing-text | On Windows a removal guard gets the system's words for a missing name, where Unix's were expected (`an_operand_is_removed_as_it_was_typed`: "The system cannot find the file specified."). Found by GitHub CI run 45520218555, 2026-11-25; `combined-r42` saw it too | `[~]` MEDIUM; fixed (the guard expects the system's own words for the missing name; the code under it unchanged); awaiting review | `.review/findings/ci-windows-missing-text.md` |
808:| ci-macos-case-mirror | On macOS a case-note guard reads "no fold" where its mirror never made the file it asks about (`the_line_claims_only_what_the_mirror_then_does`: `["[Aa]"], as typed: b`). Found by GitHub CI run 45520218555, 2026-11-25; `combined-r42` saw it too | `[~]` MEDIUM; fixed (the guard asks the filesystem whether it folds case, not a file the mirror may leave out; the code under it unchanged); awaiting review | `.review/findings/ci-macos-case-mirror.md` |
